54,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
55,3,1,talk,Asuka,,Plan A is impossible. The late-turn pool is already empty. We cannot extend hours when we can barely cover the current ones.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Takumi,,Plan B is the only one that actually addresses the mid-city time loss. It cuts through the congestion and fixes the flow.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Plan D is tempting, but given the current contractor fees, I fear it's a Trojan horse for more long-term debt.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Plan D is a path we have walked before. Eight years ago, the pilots showed it was technically sound and welcomed.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Plan C is a nightmare of pre-war shells and unrecorded voids. Plan D depends on the joint operators, who are not speaking.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, Plan B requires drivers. If we can't run the trains, we can't run a new bus corridor without cutting elsewhere.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Takumi,,Then we cut the useless stuff! I'm not voting for a fare system that doesn't actually make the trip faster.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Servas, a ""technically sound"" pilot is often a financial disaster when scaled. I prefer the tangible nature of Plan B.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Servas,,"It is true, scaling is a challenge. But the wisdom of the past tells us that the public desires simplicity in their journey.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"If we choose Plan D, the ferry and rail offices will likely clash over the data terms again. It will stall indefinitely.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"Plan C is the safest from a staffing perspective. It's a construction project, not an operational burden.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"Safe is just another word for ""doing nothing about the time loss."" I'm sticking with Plan B.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,Plan B is the most rational. It addresses the mid-city congestion without committing us to a decade of software licensing fees.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Servas,,"I see the tension. However, the accessibility records show that physical rebuilds often fail to stay in use. Plan D is a digital bridge.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,Plan C is a gamble with the ground. Plan D is a gamble with the committee. Plan B seems the most grounded.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"I cannot support A. I will vote for B if it means we don't add to the crew's burden, but C is the most logical.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,Takumi,,Stop overthinking it. B fixes the time. That's all that matters.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I believe we have reached a consensus of sorts. Plan B is the least likely to bleed us dry in the long run.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Servas,,Let us cast our votes with a spirit of hope and a mind for the future.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I will vote for the plan that is least likely to be stopped by a hidden void or a silent committee.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
